Hi Breno,

This makes sense and seems to be consistent with the intuition I was developing about my situation. I need to think about reducing the number of conditions. To some extent, it's not straightforward as they are guided by literature with regards to their potential relevance. At the same time, there may be a way to assess their relative importance across separate groups/analyses. It wouldn't be perfect in that it could not solve the entire scenario. But it could be an interesting way to assess, within a specific dimension, the relevance of the 4 or 5 conditions that refer to that dimension.

With regards to the use mvQCA, I have not specific preference at this point. It seems that it is a bit more "controversial" than the other two approaches, and I understand how that could be. And the idea of dummy variables was based on a source I had read where the authors used the red light example so say that instead of using 3 values, we could code orange and red as 1 and green as 0, if that made sense in the context of the question being asked. So I just tried it out for "fun".

Thanks for the info, this helps me think about this further!

Greg

